// A SYNTHETIC ALIAS TABLE, so that D1-16's rename path is exercised by real rows built by the real
// macro. Stage 2 T18, decision D2-10; Stage 1 guide case A-26.
//
// ===============================================================================================
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN THREE ROWS IN core/cmd/ActionId.def
// ===============================================================================================
//
// `RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS` has been live code with **zero rows** since Stage 1 T3. Nothing has been
// renamed, so nothing has ever resolved through it -- and D1-16's whole argument is that the FIRST
// rename must not be the first exercise of the path, because by then a user's saved shortcuts and
// Quick Access Toolbar entries are what is at stake.
//
// Adding rows to the shipped `ActionId.def` would fix that by writing fiction into a table whose
// strings are persisted to users' settings files: an alias says *this name used to mean that
// action*, and none of them did. So the rows live here, the resolution algorithm takes its tables
// as spans (`actionFromStringIdIn`, `core/cmd/ActionId.h`), and the shipped functions are one-line
// wrappers over the shipped arrays.
//
// ===============================================================================================
// THE ROWS, AND WHAT EACH ONE IS FOR
// ===============================================================================================
//
// Each names a REAL enumerator, so the fixture cannot drift away from the action table: delete
// `ViewToggleSidebar` and this file stops compiling, which is the correct outcome and is exactly
// what a table of dead strings would not do.
//
//   1. An ordinary retirement -- an old spelling that must still resolve, and must report as
//      retired so a settings rewrite can migrate the key while still honouring the old name on the
//      next load.
//   2. A SECOND alias onto the SAME action. Renaming twice is the ordinary shape of a long-lived
//      product, and both spellings have to keep working; a mechanism that only tolerates one hop is
//      one nobody discovers until the second rename.
//   3. A row whose old spelling COLLIDES with a live id. This is the one that cannot be written in
//      the shipped table at all, and it is the case the resolution ORDER exists for: live table
//      first, alias table second. Without a row like this, "a live id wins over an alias of the
//      same spelling" is a sentence no test can distinguish from its opposite.

// 1. The ordinary retirement.
RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS("view.sidebar.toggle", ViewToggleSidebar)

// 2. A second, older spelling of the same action -- two renames deep.
RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS("shell.toggleSidebar", ViewToggleSidebar)

// 3. THE COLLISION. `file.open` is a live id; this row claims it as a retired spelling of something
//    else entirely. Resolution must ignore it and answer `FileOpen`, because a live action always
//    outranks an alias -- and `actionStringIdIsRetiredIn` must answer false for the same reason.
RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS("file.open", ViewFullScreen)

// Undefined unconditionally, exactly as `core/cmd/ActionId.def` ends. An includer defines the macro
// it wants, includes this, and gets a clean slate back -- without which the second include in
// `tst_actionalias.cpp` warns about a redefinition, which is how this line came to be written.
#undef RAWPAGE_ACTION_ALIAS
